WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.027
A Bayesian network is a model for reasoning with uncertainty.

2
00:00:04.027 --> 00:00:10.873
It represents variables whose states are not always known, and it describes how those variables are related.

3
00:00:10.873 --> 00:00:17.317
The two main parts are a directed acyclic graph and a set of local probability distributions.

4
00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.330
The graph gives the structure.

5
00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:25.371
The probability distributions give the numbers that let the model update beliefs when evidence arrives.

6
00:00:25.371 --> 00:00:28.190
In the graph, nodes represent random variables.

7
00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:34.230
A random variable is a value that can be in different states with some uncertainty.

8
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:40.674
In an equipment example, nodes might include Power Available, Sensor Working, Alarm Active, and Equipment Fault.

9
00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:50.339
A directed edge points from one variable to another when the model treats one variable as directly relevant to the probability of the other.

10
00:00:50.339 --> 00:00:52.353
The edges are modeled dependencies.

11
00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:56.782
An edge is not automatically proof of a real-world causal relationship.

12
00:00:56.782 --> 00:00:58.796
The graph must be acyclic.

13
00:00:58.796 --> 00:01:04.031
That means you cannot follow directed arrows and return to the same node.

14
00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:07.253
Cycles would make the local probability definitions circular.

15
00:01:07.253 --> 00:01:11.280
The absence of an edge can also say something useful.

16
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:20.945
It may express an independence assumption, such as the idea that two variables do not directly affect each other once other modeled variables are

17
00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:21.348
known.

18
00:01:21.348 --> 00:01:24.569
A Bayesian network is not merely a diagram.

19
00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:29.805
The graph and probability values work together to define a complete probabilistic model.

20
00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:33.429
The graph organizes assumptions about dependency and conditional independence.

21
00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:38.664
The local distributions explain how likely each variable is under relevant parent states.

22
00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:45.108
Together, they support diagnosis, forecasting, risk analysis, security alert triage, project risk review, and system troubleshooting.

23
00:01:45.108 --> 00:01:47.121
They do not remove uncertainty.

24
00:01:47.121 --> 00:01:51.148
They make assumptions explicit enough to inspect, update, and challenge.

25
00:01:51.148 --> 00:01:54.370
That last point is important in engineering work.

26
00:01:54.370 --> 00:02:04.035
A Bayesian network is useful when the team can explain what each node means, why each edge is present, where each probability came from,

27
00:02:04.035 --> 00:02:07.660
and what evidence is allowed to update the model.

28
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.687
A clean graph with undocumented probabilities is hard to trust.

29
00:02:11.687 --> 00:02:16.519
A probability table without a clear graph is hard to reason about.

30
00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:20.949
The value comes from making the structure and numbers reviewable together.
